    prompt: "Mara Volkov, CFA, supervises a six-analyst desk under a written, well-functioning compliance manual. During a routine blotter review she notices that one analyst, Tomas, has personally bought three thinly traded names one to two days before the firm issued buy recommendations on each. Tomas tells her it was \"coincidence\" and promises it won't happen again. Under the CFA Standards, what should Volkov's first move be?",
    correct: "Recognizing this as a Standard IV(C) \"act on the red flag\" situation, promptly initiate an independent assessment of the extent of the suspected wrongdoing and place limits on or increase monitoring of Tomas's activities pending the findings, rather than accepting his explanation.",
    wrong: [
      ["Because the firm already has a documented, well-functioning compliance manual, conclude that Standard IV(C) is satisfied, note Tomas's assurance in the file, and continue routine reviews without further action.", "Treats an adequate system as a permanent shield. The existence of good procedures does not excuse inaction once a specific red flag surfaces; the supervisor's detection duty is ongoing and the recurrence of suspicious activity signals a need to act, not to rest on the manual.", "An adequate compliance system protects a supervisor from violations that occur despite reasonable efforts, but only if she still responds to red flags she actually observes. Once Volkov spots the pattern, the system has flagged the problem; the duty is now to investigate, not to close the file."],
      ["Determine that the desk's compliance system is evidently inadequate since it failed to prevent the trades, and immediately decline supervisory responsibility in writing until senior management strengthens the procedures.", "Confuses the trigger for declining supervision. Declining (and reporting an inadequate system) is the response when the absence of a workable system prevents the supervisor from discharging her duty at all, not the first move when a functioning system has surfaced a single subordinate's possible violation.", "The blotter review worked exactly as intended, so the system is not the failure here. Declining supervision is reserved for when no reasonable procedures exist to supervise through; here Volkov can and must use the working system to investigate Tomas."],
      ["Report the suspicious trades directly to the relevant securities regulator and to CFA Institute before doing anything internally, since front-running of client recommendations is a serious legal violation.", "Jumps to external reporting without first naming the supervisory duty or establishing the facts. Standard IV(C) requires the supervisor to assess the extent of the wrongdoing and contain it internally first; premature external escalation skips the required investigation and is not the standard's prescribed first step.", "The right first move is internal: investigate scope and limit the analyst's activity. External or regulatory steps may follow if the assessment confirms a violation, but they are downstream of, not a substitute for, the supervisor's own investigation."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Ask yourself whether the firm's system failed or merely did its job by surfacing the red flag, because that single distinction decides between \"decline and escalate\" and \"investigate and contain.\"",
    lesson: "This vignette turns on naming the duty before reacting. Volkov is a supervisor who has just observed a red flag: an analyst's personal trades that precede the firm's own recommendations on illiquid names, the classic fingerprint of front-running. Standard IV(C), Responsibilities of Supervisors, governs her conduct here. The Standard does two distinct things, and L2 candidates lose points by blurring them. First, it requires supervisors to establish and enforce reasonable, documented compliance procedures. Second, and separately, it requires that once a supervisor detects a possible violation, she act on it. Volkov's desk already satisfies the first prong, so the live question is purely about the second.\n\nThe \"first move\" framing matters because every distractor here jumps to an action without first classifying the situation. The Standard's guidance is explicit that relying on an employee's statements about the extent of a violation, or on assurances it will not recur, is not enough. The supervisor must promptly initiate an assessment to determine the extent of the wrongdoing and, pending the outcome, take steps to ensure it is not repeated, such as placing limits on or increasing monitoring of the employee's activities. That is precisely \"stop and investigate,\" not \"disclose and proceed.\"\n\nNotice the two traps the Standard sets for the unwary. One is the comfort of a good compliance manual: an adequate system can shield a supervisor from liability for violations that slip through despite reasonable effort, but it never licenses ignoring a red flag the supervisor personally sees. The other is the \"decline in writing\" reflex. Declining supervisory responsibility is the correct response when an absent or inadequate compliance system makes it impossible to supervise at all. Here the system worked, surfacing the very anomaly Volkov caught. The trigger for declining is simply not present, which is why that option is a tempting but incorrect mismatch of remedy to facts.",
    solution: "Standard IV(C), Responsibilities of Supervisors, imposes two duties that L2 vignettes love to test against each other.\n\n1) The build-and-enforce duty. A supervisor must make reasonable efforts to ensure that adequate compliance procedures are established, documented, communicated to covered staff, and actually followed through periodic review. If a supervisor has done this and a violation still occurs despite the reasonable system, she is generally not in violation of IV(C). Caveat: a violation occurring may itself be evidence the procedures were inadequate.\n\n2) The detect-and-respond duty. Supervisors must make reasonable efforts to detect violations, and once a red flag appears, they must act. The CFA guidance is pointed: accepting the subordinate's own account of how bad it was, or a promise that it will not happen again, is NOT sufficient. The supervisor must (a) promptly initiate an independent assessment of the extent of the wrongdoing, and (b) pending that assessment, contain the risk by limiting or increasing monitoring of the subordinate's activities.\n\nThe Whiteboard Whisperer move: before you pick an action, name the Standard and classify the situation. Is this \"disclose and proceed,\" or \"stop / investigate / escalate\"? A detected red flag of a subordinate's violation is a stop-and-investigate situation. Saying \"this is IV(C), and my first obligation is to investigate scope and contain the analyst, not to accept his explanation\" is what a documented compliance review would defend.\n\nTwo confusions to inoculate against:\n- Adequate-system-as-cure-all. A working manual protects you from the unforeseeable, not from ignoring what you have seen with your own eyes. The duty to act on red flags survives a great compliance program.\n- The decline-in-writing reflex. Declining supervisory responsibility (and flagging the system to senior management) is the remedy for an absent or inadequate system that prevents you from supervising at all. It is the wrong remedy when the system functioned and merely revealed one person's possible misconduct. Match the remedy to the trigger: inadequate system to decline/escalate; detected violation to investigate/contain.\n\nAlso resist the urge to leap straight to the regulator or CFA Institute. External reporting is downstream of, not a substitute for, the supervisor's own internal investigation. Establish the facts and contain the risk first.",
