    title: "Whose asset is the commission?",
    generated: true,
    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "Maris Okonjo, CFA, manages discretionary accounts. Her firm's compliance manual reads: \"Brokerage commissions are an asset of the client and may be spent only to benefit that client.\" A favored broker offers Okonjo a research package: equity models her clients will use, bundled with a subscription to a portfolio-accounting system the firm uses to bill clients and run payroll. The broker's commissions slightly exceed the cheapest available. What should Okonjo do to comply with Standard III(A)?",
    correct: "Direct commissions to the broker only to the extent the spending benefits the clients whose trades generate it: pay for the equity research with soft dollars, but fund the accounting/payroll system (a firm operating expense) with the firm's own money, and ensure the commission level is commensurate with the brokerage and research value received.",
    wrong: [
      ["Use the bundled package and pay for all of it with client commissions, then disclose the full arrangement, including the accounting-system component, to clients in the next quarterly statement.", "Treats disclosure as a cure for a misuse of client assets. Commissions are the client's property; using them for the firm's operating expenses (billing and payroll software) benefits the manager, not the client, so it violates the duty of loyalty no matter how transparently it is disclosed.", "Disclosure makes a permitted conflict visible; it cannot convert the client's asset into a subsidy for the firm's overhead. The operating-expense portion must be paid by the firm."],
      ["Decline the entire package and route every trade to the absolute lowest-commission broker, since paying any commission above the cheapest available breaches the duty to obtain best execution.", "Confuses 'best execution' with 'lowest commission.' Best execution maximizes portfolio value within client objectives; paying a modestly higher commission for genuine research that benefits clients is permitted when commensurate with the value received. Refusing useful client-benefiting research can itself shortchange clients.", "The test is client benefit and commensurate value, not the single cheapest quote. The client-research portion may justify a slightly higher commission."],
      ["Use client commissions for the whole bundle because Okonjo sincerely intends the accounting system to improve service to clients indirectly, and better firm infrastructure ultimately helps everyone she manages.", "Substitutes most-correct intention for most-correct action. Good intentions and vague indirect benefit do not make an operating expense a client benefit. The accounting/payroll system is firm overhead; spending the client's commissions on it benefits the manager and breaches Standard III(A).", "Standard III(A) judges the actual use of the asset, not the manager's motive. Indirect 'everyone benefits' reasoning is exactly how the invariant gets violated."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Ask \"whose asset is the commission, and does this exact spending benefit that client?\" Research the client uses qualifies; software that runs the firm's billing and payroll is the firm's own expense.",
    lesson: "The durable principle here never changes: brokerage commissions are an asset of the client, not the manager, so they may be spent only in ways that benefit the clients whose trading generates them. Everything else in the vignette is a rationalization that tries to bend around that invariant. Once you anchor on \"whose asset is this,\" the answer almost writes itself: research that the clients will actually use is a permissible soft-dollar purchase; software the firm uses to bill clients and run payroll is a firm operating expense, and paying for it with client commissions quietly converts the client's property into a subsidy for the manager's overhead.\n\nThe mixed, or bundled, package is the trap. The standard does not force an all-or-nothing choice. The right move is to unbundle: pay the client-benefiting research component with soft dollars and pay the operating-expense component with the firm's own money. This is the same logic the standard applies to \"mixed-use\" items, but the question is framed so you reason from the principle rather than recall a label. Note also the separate best-execution thread: best execution seeks to maximize the value of the client's portfolio, which is not the same as always paying the lowest possible commission. A modestly higher commission tied to genuine, client-benefiting research can be acceptable when it is commensurate with the brokerage and research value received.\n\nWatch how each distractor fails a different way candidates actually fail. One leans on disclosure as a cure-all, but disclosure cannot legitimize spending the client's asset on the firm's overhead. One leans on a sincere intention that better infrastructure \"helps everyone,\" substituting most-correct intention for most-correct action. One over-corrects into \"lowest commission is best execution,\" confusing a price floor with the duty to maximize portfolio value. A documented compliance review would defend exactly one action: spend the commission only where it benefits the client, and have the firm pay for its own operating expenses.",
    solution: "Standard III(A), Loyalty, Prudence, and Care, governs how a manager handles client assets, and brokerage commissions are explicitly treated as an asset of the client. That single fact is the invariant that controls every soft-dollar and directed-brokerage question: the commission belongs to the client, so it may be used only to benefit the client whose trades produced it.\n\nFrom that invariant flow three working rules. (1) Client-benefit / commensurate-value test: soft dollars may buy research or brokerage services that benefit clients, and the commission paid must be commensurate with the value of those services. Paying a higher commission to obtain goods or services with no corresponding benefit to the client violates the duty of loyalty. (2) Operating-expense prohibition: client commissions may not pay the manager's own operating expenses, things like office rent, billing software, or payroll systems, because those benefit the manager, not the client. (3) Mixed-use items: when a product has both a client-benefiting component and a firm-benefiting component, the manager allocates, paying the client-benefit portion with soft dollars and the firm-benefit portion with firm money.\n\nBest execution is a related but distinct duty. It is defined as a trading process that seeks to maximize the value of the client's portfolio within stated objectives and constraints, not simply the lowest commission. A slightly higher commission can be consistent with best execution if it secures genuinely valuable, client-benefiting research or service.\n\nDirected brokerage adds a wrinkle worth knowing for the exam even though it is not the crux here: when a client directs the manager to use a particular broker, the manager may comply, but must inform the client that this directed arrangement may prevent the manager from obtaining best price and execution, and should obtain the client's written acknowledgment. Disclosure is the right tool for that permitted conflict, but disclosure is never a tool for laundering a misuse of the client's asset. The exam's favorite mistakes cluster here: treating disclosure as a cure-all when the real fix is to avoid or correct the misuse, substituting the manager's good intentions for the actual use of the asset, and forgetting that you can unbundle a mixed-use product instead of choosing all-or-nothing.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "A broker bundles equity research your clients will use with a subscription to software your firm uses to bill clients and run payroll, all paid via brokerage commissions that run a bit above the cheapest quote. Under Standard III(A), how should the manager fund each component to keep faith with the rule that commissions are the client's asset?" },
