    id: 8493003,
    chapter: "CFA L2 Ethics",
    title: "The client's performance bonus",
    generated: true,
    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "Priya Anand manages the Calloway family account at Marin Advisors. Over dinner, the Calloways offer her a personal bonus: 1% of any gains above a 10% annual return, paid directly to her, on top of her Marin salary. They want to \"keep it between us\" so colleagues won't feel slighted. Priya is flattered and confident she can outperform. Under Standard IV(B), what should Priya do before this arrangement can stand?",
    correct: "Decline the arrangement unless she first obtains written consent from both the Calloways and Marin Advisors, since the contingent client bonus could create a conflict with her employer's interests.",
    wrong: [
      ["Accept now, then draft a memo to Marin's compliance team within 30 days describing the bonus terms, the performance hurdle, and her plan to allocate trades fairly across all clients despite the incentive.", "This is the elaborate self-justification that swaps most-correct ACTION for after-the-fact disclosure. Standard IV(B) requires written consent from all parties BEFORE accepting; a detailed retroactive memo plus a fairness 'plan' does not cure acceptance of a conflicting arrangement that was never authorized.", "Consent is a precondition, not a follow-up. The question is not how thoroughly you explain it later, but whether every party agreed in writing first."],
      ["Accept the bonus but route it into an escrow account and disclose it to Marin only if the account's return actually exceeds the 10% hurdle, since no conflict exists unless the bonus is ever earned.", "This is the technically arguable but wrong call: the conflict arises from the incentive's existence, not from whether it eventually pays out. The mere prospect of a private contingent reward can distort risk-taking and client prioritization the moment it is accepted, so escrow and conditional disclosure miss the point.", "The conflict is the incentive, not the payout. Ask what could bias your judgment today, not what hits your bank account next year."],
      ["Verbally inform her supervisor at Marin about the offer and accept it, because she already receives an employer bonus and one more performance incentive aligned with client gains poses no new conflict.", "Two errors: verbal notice fails the written-consent requirement (consent must be documentable, e.g., email), and an existing employer bonus does not absorb a separately negotiated, undisclosed client payment. Aligned-sounding incentives can still skew loyalty and objectivity.", "'Aligned' is not 'authorized,' and a hallway conversation is not a record. If it cannot be retrieved and documented, it is not consent."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Ask whether the arrangement was authorized in writing by everyone affected BEFORE it was accepted; thoroughness of later explanation never substitutes for advance consent.",
    lesson: "Standard IV(B), Additional Compensation Arrangements, says a member must not accept gifts, benefits, compensation, or consideration that competes with, or might reasonably be expected to create a conflict of interest with, the employer's interest unless written consent is obtained from all parties involved. The trigger is potential conflict, and the cure is consent secured in advance from everyone affected, which here means both the Calloways and Marin Advisors.\n\nThe elegant shortcut here is to stop treating the situation as a disclosure problem to be managed and instead treat it as an authorization problem to be resolved before anything else happens. A contingent, performance-linked payment from one client, paid privately to the manager, is a textbook conflict: it gives Priya a personal financial reason to favor the Calloway account, chase return above a hurdle, or take on risk that may not suit that client or others. The fact that she is confident she can outperform, or that the incentive sounds aligned with client gains, does not remove the conflict; it is exactly the kind of pull on loyalty and objectivity the Standard targets.\n\nNote also what \"written\" and \"all parties\" mean. Written consent is any form of communication that can be documented and retrieved, such as email; a verbal heads-up does not qualify. \"All parties\" includes the employer, whose interest the arrangement could undercut, not merely the client offering the money. And timing is decisive: the consent must precede acceptance. The wrong answers are attractive because each builds an elaborate scaffolding (a compliance memo, an escrow mechanism, an existing-bonus rationale) around an action that was never authorized in the first place. The simple defensible move is to withhold acceptance until the written sign-offs exist.",
    solution: "Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements: A member or candidate must not accept compensation, gifts, benefits, or consideration that competes with, or might reasonably be expected to create a conflict of interest with, the employer's interest UNLESS they obtain written consent from all parties involved.\n\nHow to apply it at L2 vignette depth:\n1) Spot the trigger. Any outside payment, gift, or perk tied to the work you do for your employer or that could bias your loyalty/objectivity is in scope. Performance-linked bonuses from a client are a classic example because they reward favoring that client.\n2) The cure is consent, obtained IN ADVANCE. The sequence matters: get sign-off, then (maybe) accept. Accepting first and explaining later is a violation no matter how thorough the later explanation.\n3) \"Written\" means documentable and retrievable (email counts; a verbal conversation does not).\n4) \"All parties\" means everyone whose interests are affected, at minimum the employer AND the client/third party making the offer. Telling only one side is insufficient.\n5) The conflict lives in the incentive's existence, not its payout. An arrangement you \"might\" benefit from still distorts judgment from day one, so conditioning disclosure on whether the bonus is eventually earned fails.\n\nStricter-rule reminder: if firm policy or local law is stricter than IV(B) (for example, banning client gratuities outright), follow the stricter rule. Disclosure does not cure a conflict that policy says to avoid entirely.\n\nContrast with the de minimis idea: a small token gift of immaterial value may not require consent, but a contingent cash arrangement linked to performance is plainly material and conflict-creating, so it does.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "A wealth manager is privately offered a side payment by one of her clients, calculated as a slice of any returns above a set threshold and paid on top of her firm salary, with a request to keep it quiet from coworkers. Which single step does Standard IV(B) require before such an arrangement is permissible?" },
